│ Reasoning:                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│ The core issue is that FileInput incorrectly displays a required   │
│ attribute even when a file has already been set. This happens      │
│ because ClearableFileInput’s use_required_attribute() method is    │
│ being called on FileInput when it shouldn’t be. The fix is to move │
│ the logic from ClearableFileInput to FileInput itself, ensuring    │
│ that the required attribute is only set when a file is not already │
│ associated with the form field.                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ Patch:                                                             │
│                                                                    │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃                         modification 1                         ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  [file][file]django/forms/widgets.py[/file]                        │
│  [original] def use_required_attribute(self, filename=None):       │
│      if filename is not None:                                      │
│          self.fields[filename].required = True                     │
│  [/original]                                                       │
│  [patched] def use_required_attribute(self, filename=None):        │
│      if filename is not None and not self.has_file():              │
│          self.fields[filename].required = True                     │
│  [/patched]                                                        │
